Lab 13: Configuring and Monitoring Camel Routes
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Monitor Camel routes using built-in tools and logs
Integrate Camel with Prometheus and Grafana for advanced monitoring
Configure alerts for route failures and performance issues
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of Apache Camel and Java
JDK 11+ installed
Apache Camel 3.x (standalone or Spring Boot)
Maven installed
Docker installed (for Prometheus/Grafana)
Basic Linux command line knowledge
Lab Setup
Create a new Camel project:
mvn archetype:generate \
  -DarchetypeGroupId=org.apache.camel.archetypes \
  -DarchetypeArtifactId=camel-archetype-spring-boot \
  -DarchetypeVersion=3.20.0
Navigate to the project directory:
cd camel-spring-boot-project
Task 1: Monitoring Routes with Camel's Built-in Tools
Subtask 1.1: Enable Route Logging
Open src/main/resources/application.properties and add:
# Enable Camel logging
camel.springboot.main-run-controller=true
logging.level.org.apache.camel=DEBUG
Create a simple route in src/main/java/com/example/MyRouteBuilder.java:
from("timer:foo?period=5000")
  .routeId("monitored-route")
  .log("Route executed at ${header.firedTime}")
  .to("log:route.output");
Run the application:
mvn spring-boot:run
Expected Outcome: You should see detailed logs in the console showing route execution every 5 seconds.

Subtask 1.2: Use JMX for Monitoring
Add JMX configuration to application.properties:
# Enable JMX
camel.context.mbean-export.enabled=true
Install JConsole (comes with JDK) and connect to your running Camel application.
Troubleshooting Tip: If JMX doesn't connect, ensure your Java process has these JVM options:

-Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.port=1099 -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.ssl=false -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.authenticate=false
Task 2: Integrate with Prometheus and Grafana
Subtask 2.1: Add Prometheus Metrics
Add dependencies to pom.xml:
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
  <artifactId>camel-micrometer</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
  <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
  <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
</dependency>
Configure metrics in application.properties:
# Enable Prometheus metrics
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,metrics,prometheus
management.endpoint.prometheus.enabled=true
Modify your route to include metrics:
from("timer:foo?period=5000")
  .routeId("monitored-route")
  .to("micrometer:counter:route.counter")
  .log("Route executed at ${header.firedTime}");
Subtask 2.2: Set Up Prometheus and Grafana
Create prometheus.yml:
global:
  scrape_interval: 5s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'camel'
    metrics_path: '/actuator/prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['host.docker.internal:8080']
Run Prometheus and Grafana:
docker run -d -p 9090:9090 -v $(pwd)/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml prom/prometheus
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 grafana/grafana
Access Prometheus at http://localhost:9090 and Grafana at http://localhost:3000

In Grafana:

Add Prometheus as data source (URL: http://host.docker.internal:9090)
Import Camel dashboard (ID: 11600)
Expected Outcome: You should see Camel metrics visualized in Grafana.

Task 3: Configure Alerts
Subtask 3.1: Set Up Route Failure Alerts
Modify your route to include error handling:
from("timer:foo?period=5000")
  .routeId("monitored-route")
  .doTry()
    .process(exchange -> {
      // Simulate occasional failure
      if (System.currentTimeMillis() % 3 == 0) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Simulated error");
      }
    })
  .doCatch(Exception.class)
    .to("micrometer:counter:route.errors")
    .log("Error occurred: ${exception.message}")
  .end();
Subtask 3.2: Create Grafana Alert
In Grafana:
Navigate to Alert -> Notification channels
Add your preferred channel (Email, Slack, etc.)
Create a new dashboard panel with query: increase(route_errors_total[1m]) > 0
Set alert threshold and notification
Expected Outcome: You'll receive alerts when route errors exceed threshold.

Conclusion
In this lab, you learned to:

Monitor Camel routes using built-in logging and JMX
Integrate Camel with Prometheus and Grafana for advanced metrics visualization
Configure alerts for route failures and performance issues
These skills are essential for maintaining production-grade Camel applications and are particularly valuable for Red Hat OpenShift Development certification.

